est related tests; if the result differs from the hypothesis, inspect the new failure and iterate rather than declaring success. Trace the relevant data and control flow, state the likely root cause, and identify the smallest compatible change. Before implementation, enumerate the behavioral contract for valid, empty, null, missing, malformed, boundary, repeated, reordered, and unsupported inputs, including expected exceptions, defaults, mutation, ordering, precision, and backward compatibility. Make the patch satisfy the contract without broadening behavior unintentionally. Then build a behavior matrix before editing and turn each row into a validation obligation. Execute validation in layers: focused regression test, adjacent unit tests, affected subsystem tests, and the broadest practical suite. For every failure, classify it as an implementation defect, an incomplete test expectation, an environment issue, or an unrelated pre-existing failure, and continue debugging implementation defects until the focused and adjacent checks pass. normal inputs; empty, null, missing, malformed, and partially specified inputs; minimum, maximum, zero, negative, overflow, underflow, and exact-boundary values; subclasses and alternate implementations; omitted versus explicit defaults; expected exceptions and messages; and interactions with existing public APIs. For every row, compare the old and new behavior explicitly, including return values, exception types and messages, ordering, mutation, numerical precision, serialization, and subclass or alternate-backend behavior. Mark each difference as required or accidental, add a focused regression test for every required difference, and add compatibility tests for nearby unchanged rows while preserving unrelated behavior. After every nontrivial edit, run the narrowest relevant test or a focused reproduction, inspect the complete failure output, and iterate on the implementation until the focused checks pass. Then run the broader relevant test groups and, before finalizing, perform an adversarial compatibility review covering public API signatures, exception types and messages, ordering, mutability, numerical precision, serialization, subclass behavior, and interactions with neighboring features; reject any change lacking evidence for intentional behavior differences.
